AIRK Service @@VERSION@@ â€” macOS @@ARCH@@

This disk image installs AIRK Service for your account: the airk service and
the airkctl command-line client inside AIRK.app. It contains no VSIX and does
not install or open VS Code; the VS Code extension is a separate download.

Open "Install AIRK.command" to start setup in Terminal. No administrator
password is required. It verifies the disk image payload and the Developer ID
signature of AIRK.app before it runs anything, asks for the program folder and
the index data, cache and log locations, and shows what AIRK setup found.
Every earlier AIRK installation is stopped and removed with its indexes, which
must be rebuilt; no source file is read, changed or deleted, and coding agents
must be reconnected. Setup asks before it proceeds; an unattended run (--silent; --yes remains an alias)
needs --acknowledge-reset when an earlier installation is found.

  Program folder  ~/Library/Application Support/AIRK/program
                  (AIRK.app, notices/, the install record)
  Index data      ~/Library/Application Support/AIRK/data/indexes
  Service state   ~/Library/Application Support/AIRK/state/service
  Cache           ~/Library/Caches/AIRK/service
  Logs            ~/Library/Logs/AIRK/service

Interactive login startup uses AIRK.app's SMAppService agent. macOS may require
approval in System Settings; setup reports login_consent_required and exits
nonzero until approval is granted. Approve it, then resume the same retained
request. --no-login-startup skips registration; "airkctl service start" starts
AIRK manually after a later login. Certificate trust for the HTTPS
admin pages is offered through the macOS consent dialog. When ~/.local/bin
exists, setup adds an airkctl link there and records that it owns it;
otherwise it prints the airkctl path. No shell startup file is edited.

Running "Install AIRK.command" again repairs or upgrades the installation in
place and keeps the indexes. To uninstall, run:

  airkctl service uninstall --yes                  # keeps index data
  airkctl service uninstall --yes --purge-indexes  # also deletes it

Uninstall removes the install record and pointer, owned certificate trust,
the placed files and the owned airkctl link. Eject this disk image after setup.

The disk image and AIRK.app use Ioka LLC's Developer ID Application signature
and Apple notarization. This package contains no account credentials.

Headless --silent never reads stdin or asks for certificate trust. Trust is
skipped; request it later through the native interactive consent flow. A setup
or PATH/link failure exits nonzero and prints a persistent private recovery
folder containing the original request and verified setup image. Preserve it
and check service status before attempting recovery. Resume with the exact same
installer payload and original request:

  ./"Install AIRK.command" --resume "<absolute retained operation-request.json>"

Add --silent only when the original operation did not request consent. Do not
supply new locations/options, edit the JSON or replace it with a new request.
This also resumes an original native uninstall request retained by airkctl;
completed native work is reconciled before remaining wrapper work runs.
The portable CLI --resume command is reserved for portable setup requests.
Unicode paths are accepted.

Headless installation skips login startup; AIRK starts once. Use the supported
manual startup path after login. --no-login-startup also makes that choice
explicit in an interactive installation.
